<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    Entitlements for the hardened runtime, which notarisation requires.

    The app is deliberately NOT sandboxed: it records to whatever volume the
    operator chooses, runs gst-launch, and installs a panel into Adobe's
    extensions folder. A sandbox would break all three.
  -->

  <!--
    GStreamer is loaded from Homebrew, signed by a different team, and it
    dlopen()s a plugin per element at runtime. Under the hardened runtime,
    library validation would refuse every one of those, so the in-process
    engine could not start a pipeline at all.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    GStreamer reads GST_PLUGIN_PATH, GST_REGISTRY and friends from the
    environment to find its plugins and scanner.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    ORC is GStreamer's SIMD JIT, used by videoconvert, audioconvert and friends.
    It maps memory write+execute at runtime, which the hardened runtime forbids
    by default. Measured: without these the app signs and launches perfectly,
    connects, decodes, and shows live audio meters — and writes ZERO bytes,
    because ORC fails inside the converters and the pipeline stalls:

      ORC: ERROR: orc_code_region_allocate_codemem(): Failed to create write and
      exec mmap regions. This is probably because the Hardened Runtime is enabled.

    allow-jit covers MAP_JIT allocations; allow-unsigned-executable-memory
    covers the plain RWX mmap ORC actually uses. Both, because which one applies
    depends on how ORC was built.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    Recordings usually land on an external RAID or a mounted share rather than
    in the home folder.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
